# Chroma content pack, The Five Callings: THE INVENTION ROAD (Blue, the inventor), moments 1 to 9 of
# callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, "The invention road" (Pathways and packs thread, 2026-10-19, for v24; switched off until
# `callings` is in batch.PACKS). Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: chroma-library/SPEC.md,
# notes/library-spec.md, the footprints brief (chroma-library/drafts/footprints-brief.md) and callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md
# (pack rules, calibration lessons, balance layout). Names in title:, grants:, takes:, requires:, holds: and aims: come
# from callings/catalogue.py, core/*.py and the base catalogue (chroma-library/earth_perks_titles.py); the world lines
# follow the forms of callings/worlds.py.
#
# The road climbs from the base trades ([software developer], [civil engineer], [machinist], [electrician], [founder
# of a firm], [apprentice]) to [inventor], the Known rung, and to [celebrated inventor], the summit, a facet on
# [inventor]. A tendency, never a wall: every colour has its honest way up every rung (White's rule and the test done to
# the letter, Blue's study and the clever design, Black's backers and the deal, Red's risky prototype and the night at
# the bench, Green's old machine mended and the street that gets the first ones).
#   1-2  everyday pair U-A, held by the trades: calls UR / calls WBG, cross cycle s1
#   3-4  everyday pair U-B, held by [inventor]: calls UG / calls WBR, cross cycle s3
#   5    step: the prototype works (pairs D2a): [inventor] at 25
#   6    step: an invention in every home (pairs D2b): [celebrated inventor] at 6, with [an invention in everyday use]
#   7    fall and wrong: the safety test you could skip (triads D1b)
#   8    long shot: the garage invention (triads D4a): [inventor] at 3
#   9    world-only, only: magic: the guild commissions a flying ship (pairs D6b)
#
# Holder pairs (calibration lesson 1) use aims: and never give a new title: rate .5, gap 1-3, no share: line (the holds:
# gate does the drawing); grants: gives only a perk plainly earned. The two halves of a pair share stages, ages, holds:,
# rate and gap, and both use the cycle named on the pair's header line. Life events are each even on their own: alpha
# even, stakes 1.0, the five one-colour options plus the combination half named in their header comment (build.py D
# and PAIRSETS). Chances are set so every colour's mean is the same inside each moment (a pair counts half to each of
# its colours, a triad a third), so the file is even in every stage. Steps carry their real chances (the brief's pack
# rules), the same for every colour that tries for the same title inside a moment, and no perk on failure; the earned-
# odds lifts (helps.py, half on the summit) come on top in the engine. The long shot's five singles carry
# `grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed` (core/longshot.py). per_year is the brief's: the yearly rate of meeting the
# moment per eligible person, inside the gates of engine-conditions.py (LIFE_CALLINGS, LIFE_CALLINGS_LONGSHOTS);
# drivers are the brief's, except the fall (7), whose drivers are this file's own reading. Every option of a life event
# leaves one or two footprints: a field it already has (a title, a perk of a kind the footprint check reads, a mark the
# rules read) or `foot:` just before `chance:`; the everyday moments need none.
#
# Wrongs are pickable with plain labels and no preaching, each marked and closed with a real backfire, spread over the
# colours: Red's idea built on the firm's time with the firm's parts (1, law), Green's parts taken from a machine already
# sold for scrap (2, law), Black's patent sold to be shelved behind a partner's back (3, approval), Blue's fault kept
# from the backers (4, approval), the triad UBR's safety test skipped (7, law), the pair UB's enchanter's binding bought
# without the Order's leave (9, approval). White's line locked off against the manager's word (2) is a closed act of
# defiance, not a wrong.
#
# Content: a failed test stays plain and offstage (smoke, a small fire put out, a burnt hand under the cold tap); the
# customer hurt in the skipped test's backfire is one plain clause; no child is harmed in any text, and children appear
# only in safe forms (a family at the kitchen table, the band's young ones carrying a toy about). No real person, firm,
# brand, product, place, country or year is named: "the big firm", "the trade fair", "the engineers' journal". In the
# magic world the binding that makes the ship rise is an enchanter's, bought, with the Order's disapproval on a closed:
# approval line and its price in the backfire; nothing rests on the person's own gift. Modern Earth has no magic. Every
# moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines (the world-only moment its own world's alone) and an untagged scene
# for each other world; an option whose act reads differently there carries its own wording; the works and fails lines
# read in every world.

# ================================================================ everyday pair U-A: the trades (cycle s1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. the idea in the shed at night (calls UR)
# An idea that will not let go, built at night from parts. [prototyping] for the one who builds it properly; aims:
# inventor on the careful build and the summer saved up for. The wrong is Red's: the idea built on the firm's time with
# the firm's parts, and nothing said (hid a wrong; closed: law; backfire: the firm claims the idea). Every colour's mean
# is 70.

== the idea in the shed at night
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per software developer, civil engineer, machinist, electrician, founder of a firm or apprentice a year: about 1 in 6 works at an idea of their own in the evenings for a while (estimate); about 1 life in 5 holds one of these trades at some time, and about 5 lives in 10,000 come to live from their own inventions (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
calls: UR
holds: software developer | civil engineer | machinist | electrician | founder of a firm | apprentice
tone: joy
life: work, making
horizon: months
roles: partner, boss, mentor
earth: an idea that will not let go, and the shed at the bottom of the garden: a bench, a lamp and a box of old parts
tribal: an idea for a new kind of tool that will not let go, worked at by the embers after the band sleeps
magic: an idea for a new device that will not let go, built at night in the workshop's back room from odds and ends
scene: It started as a sketch on the back of a work rota, and now it will not leave {N} alone. Most nights after supper {N} goes down to the shed at the bottom of the garden, switches on the lamp over the bench and tips out the box of old parts: small motors, springs, the circuit board from a broken washing machine. {partner} has stopped asking when {N} is coming to bed.
scene W: Somewhere in {Ns} contract at work there is a clause about ideas. Before the next screw goes in, {N} would like to know exactly whose idea this is, and to have it written down.
scene U: The thing almost works on paper. There is one joint that will not take the load, and {N} has drawn it eleven ways in the notebook, each a little closer.
scene B: {N} keeps doing the sums in the dark: what it would cost to make, what a shop would charge, who would buy the first thousand.
scene R: {N} cannot sleep for thinking about it. The urge is to build the whole thing tonight, all at once, and watch it move.
scene G: The shed smells of oil and cut wood, and half the parts on the bench came from things {Ns} grandfather mended and never threw away. Somewhere in that box is everything the idea needs.
scene tribal: When the band sleeps, {N} sits by the embers turning a new kind of tool over and over: a scraper with a bone grip that will not slip, a hook that holds what the old ones lose. It will not leave {N} alone, and the others laugh at the heap of broken tries by the shelter.
scene magic: Most nights, when the workshop has closed, {N} takes a lamp into the back room and works at a device of {Ns} own: brass wheels, a spring from a broken clock, a lens from the glazier's scraps. The master does not know, and {N} has not decided whether to say.
- W | read what your contract says about ideas, and keep a dated notebook of every step | v: conformity, security | tribal: ask the elders whether a new tool belongs to its maker or the band, and keep to it | magic: read the guild's rules on new devices, and keep a dated book of every step | chance: 80
- U | draw it, model it, and build the first working part properly | grants: prototyping | aims: inventor | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: shape it in soft wood first, then in bone, trying each part in turn | magic: draw it, build a small model, then make the first working part in brass | chance: 60
- B | take a rough one to the Saturday market every week, and see what people pay | habit | v: achievement, power | tribal: take a rough one to every trading meet, and see what people would give for it | magic: take a rough one to the market every week, and see what people pay | chance: 70
- R | build it on the firm's time, with the firm's parts, and say nothing | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: an employer's design taken; backfire: the firm finds out, claims the idea as its own, and the patent with it | self_control: - | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: make it from the band's own store of good flint, and say nothing | magic: build it on the master's time, with the master's brass, and say nothing | chance: 70
- G | build it from what is already in the shed: old motors, offcuts, a mended vice | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: make it from what is already by the shelter: old antler, offcuts of bone, worn stones | magic: build it from what is already in the back room: old wheels, offcuts, a mended vice | chance: 80
- W>U | keep two evenings a week for it, every week, and never more | self_control: + | habit | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: keep two evenings of every moon for it, and never more | chance: 60
- U>B | look up every patent near the idea, and find the gap nobody owns yet | v: achievement, power | tribal: ask the traders which tools the far bands already make, and find the one nobody makes | magic: search the guild's register of designs, and find the gap nobody holds yet | chance: 80
- B>R | save up for the parts first, then give yourself one summer to make it run | aims: inventor | self_control: + | v: self-direction, achievement | tribal: put by furs for good stone first, then give yourself one summer to make it work | chance: 70
- R>G | build it at the kitchen table every weekend, with the whole family's hands in it | habit | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: make it at the hearth every evening, with the whole family's hands in it | magic: build it at the kitchen table every feast day, with the whole household's hands in it | chance: 70
- G>W | turn it to the allotments' broken water pump, built as the plot holders need it | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: turn it to the band's leaking water skins, made as those who carry the water need it | magic: turn it to the ward's broken well pump, built as the neighbours need it | chance: 60
works: The idea takes shape, and one night it moves the way {N} saw it move in {Ns} head.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the idea is further on by the spring, and the notebook is full of it.
fails: The part that would not take the load still will not, and the tries are put away for the winter.
fails: Work and sleep win, the evenings go to other things for weeks at a time, and the idea cools.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. the machine nobody can fix (calls WBG)
# At work the old machine everyone depends on stops, and its maker is long gone. [prototyping] for the new part made
# from the old one's drawings, [fixing things] for the manual written and the old fitter's tricks learned; aims: inventor
# on the new part and on the replacement pitched to the boss. White's lock-off against the manager's word is a closed act
# (defied an authority); the wrong is Green's: parts taken from the old twin in the scrapyard, already sold (hid a wrong;
# closed: law). Every colour's mean is 70.

== the machine nobody can fix
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per software developer, civil engineer, machinist, electrician, founder of a firm or apprentice a year: about 1 in 5 sees an old machine or system the work depends on fail with nobody left who knows it well (estimate); about 1 life in 5 holds one of these trades at some time (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
calls: WBG
holds: software developer | civil engineer | machinist | electrician | founder of a firm | apprentice
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, mentor
earth: the old machine the whole works depends on has stopped, and the firm that made it closed decades ago
tribal: the great fish weir across the river has given way at the middle, days before the salmon run, and nobody alive knows how it was first laid
magic: the great water-wheel that drives the guild's mill has seized, and the artificer who built its governor is fifty years dead
scene: Monday morning, and the old machine at the heart of the works has stopped: a press the size of a lorry, older than anyone on the floor, run by a grey control box nobody has opened in years. The firm that made it closed decades ago, the manual is a few stained pages, and the whole line stands still. {boss} looks round the room for someone who can fix it.
scene W: There is a right way to do this: lock it off, read what manual there is, test one thing at a time and write each one down. {N} has seen what happens when people skip the steps.
scene U: {N} has wondered for years how the old thing actually works. Now it stands open on the floor, and nobody will stop {N} finding out.
scene B: The works is losing money by the hour, and whoever gets it running will be remembered at review time. {N} knows it, and so does {colleague}.
scene R: The line is silent, the floor is full of people standing about, and {N} wants to be inside that machine with a torch and a spanner now.
scene G: The old machine has run through three generations of workers, and {mentor}, who kept it going for thirty years, retired last spring. {N} thinks of the old tricks that went out of the door with {mentor}.
scene tribal: The great fish weir across the river, built by the grandparents of the oldest elder, has given way at the middle, and the salmon run is days off. Nobody alive knows how it was first laid, and the band looks to the ones who make things.
scene magic: The great water-wheel that drives the guild's mill has seized, its clockwork governor jammed solid, and the artificer who built it has been dead fifty years. The millers stand idle, the city's flour runs low, and the guild master looks round for someone who can mend it.
- W | lock it off by the safety rules, though the manager wants it running today | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: the line stopped against the manager's word; backfire: a written warning, and the shift's pay docked for the lost day | identity | v: conformity, security | tribal: forbid anyone to wade out to the weir until it is made safe, whatever the elders say | magic: chain the wheel still by the guild's rules, though the master wants it turning today | chance: 55
- U | take it apart, draw how it works, and make a new part that does the same | grants: prototyping | aims: inventor | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: take the weir apart stake by stake, learn how it was laid, and cut a new middle | magic: take the governor apart, draw how it works, and make a new part that does the same | chance: 70
- B | tell the boss you can fix it, and name your price first | v: power, achievement | tribal: tell the elders you can mend the weir, and ask for the first fish of the run | magic: tell the guild master you can mend it, and name your fee first | chance: 65
- R | climb inside with a torch and a spanner, and get it running tonight | body: light | v: stimulation | tribal: wade out into the cold river, and lash the weir back together before dark | magic: climb into the wheel housing with a lamp and a spanner, and get it turning tonight | chance: 65
- G | visit the old fitter who kept it going, every evening, until you know it too | grants: fixing things | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: sit with the oldest weir-builder every evening, until you know how the grandparents laid it | magic: visit the old millwright who kept it turning, every evening, until you know it too | chance: 75
- W>U | write down every fault and every fix, so the next person has a manual | grants: fixing things | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: teach the young ones each stake and knot as it goes back, so the next mending is easier | magic: write down every fault and every fix, and leave the guild a proper manual | chance: 85
- U>B | design a modern replacement, and pitch it to the boss as your own project | aims: inventor | v: achievement, power | tribal: plan a better weir of your own making, and ask the elders for leave to build it | magic: design a new governor, and offer it to the guild master as your own work | chance: 70
- B>R | get the boss to pay double for a weekend crew, and lead the strip-down yourself | v: power, stimulation | tribal: promise the helpers a share of the first catch, and lead the rebuilding yourself | magic: get the guild to pay double for a feast-day crew, and lead the work yourself | chance: 75
- R>G | work through the weekend to save the old machine, and the jobs that hang on it | self_control: + | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: work day and night to save the old weir, and the winter food that hangs on it | magic: work through the feast days to save the old wheel, and the millers' bread | chance: 75
- G>W | take the parts it needs from the old twin in the scrapyard, though it is sold | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: parts taken from a machine already sold for scrap; backfire: the scrap dealer counts the gaps, sends the firm a bill, and the boss wants a name | v: security, tradition | tribal: take the stakes it needs from the old weir of the band upriver, though it is theirs | magic: take the parts it needs from the old twin wheel in the breaker's yard, though it is sold | chance: 65
works: By the end of the week the old machine is running again, and people stop by to look at it as if it were new.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the work starts again, and {N} knows the old thing better than anyone now living.
fails: The fix holds for a day and then fails again, and the whole floor waits another week for help from outside.
fails: Nothing {N} tries brings it back, and {boss} sends far away for someone who costs a great deal.

# ================================================================ everyday pair U-B: the inventor (cycle s3)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. a big firm wants to buy the patent (calls UG)
# A big firm offers to buy the patent outright, and it makes the very thing the invention would replace. [savings] for
# the sales that bring money in, [investors who back you] for the refusal that finds backers instead; aims: celebrated
# inventor on the refusal and on the cheap licence that lets it spread. The wrong is Black's: the biggest offer, from the
# firm that will shelve it, with the partner who helped kept out of it (broke your word; closed: approval). Every
# colour's mean is 70.

== a big firm wants to buy the patent
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per inventor a year: about 1 in 6 is offered money for a patent or a design, outright or as a licence, and a few of those offers come from firms that would rather the idea never reached the shops (estimate); about 5 lives in 10,000 come to live from their own inventions (catalogue share)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-95
calls: UG
holds: inventor
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: colleague, partner, boss
earth: a letter from a big firm: it wants to buy the patent outright, and it makes the very thing the invention would replace
tribal: a trader from the far bands comes with a canoe of goods for the sole right to make the new tool
magic: a great merchant house offers a purse of gold for the guild's grant on the design, and it sells the very device the design would replace
scene: The letter from the big firm is two pages long and very polite. They would like to buy {Ns} patent outright, the one {N} and {colleague} spent three years of evenings on, for more money than {N} has ever seen in one place. A smaller firm in town has rung as well. {N} knows the big firm makes the very thing the invention would replace.
scene W: There was never a written agreement with {colleague}, only a handshake over the bench. {N} thinks a handshake is still a promise.
scene U: {N} reads the offer three times, then the small print, then the patent itself. A patent runs twenty years, and the right licence could be worth more than any sale.
scene B: The number in the letter is real money, and {N} knows the big firm would pay more if pushed. Somebody is going to do well out of this, and it may as well be {N}.
scene R: Sell it, and someone in a meeting room decides what becomes of it. {N} built the thing, and the thought of handing it over makes {Ns} jaw tighten.
scene G: The small firm in town employs forty people, half of them {Ns} neighbours. {N} pictures the invention being made down the road, by hands {N} knows.
scene tribal: A trader from the far bands has come with a canoe full of goods: flints, furs and shells. The trader wants the sole right to make {Ns} new tool, for the far bands alone. {N} made it with {colleague}, over three winters by the fire.
scene magic: A great merchant house has sent its steward with a purse of gold and a contract on vellum: it would buy the guild's grant on {Ns} design outright. {N} made it with {colleague} over three years in the back room, and the merchant house sells the very device the design would replace.
- W | sell only on fair terms, read through by a lawyer, with the partner's share written in | grants: savings | mark: kept your word | v: security, conformity | tribal: trade only before the elders, with the partner's share set out for all to hear | magic: sell only on fair terms, witnessed at the guildhall, with the partner's share written in | chance: 70
- U | keep the patent, and license it to both firms for a fee on every one made | v: achievement, security | tribal: keep the making in your own hands, and let both bands make it for a gift on every one | magic: keep the guild's grant, and let both houses make it for a fee on every one made | chance: 65
- B | sell to the firm that will shelve it, for top money, and keep the partner out | grants: savings | mark: broke your word | closed: approval: a shared invention sold to be shelved, behind a partner's back; backfire: the partner finds the contract, and the whole trade hears who sold out | v: power | tribal: give the far trader the sole right to it, and tell the partner nothing until the canoe has gone | magic: sell to the house that will bury it, for the most gold, and keep the partner out | chance: 75
- R | turn them down flat, and find backers to make it yourself | grants: investors who back you | aims: celebrated inventor | binds | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: send the trader away, and find traders who will share their goods while you make it yourself | magic: turn the steward away, and find backers among the merchant houses to make it yourself | chance: 55
- G | sell it to the small firm in town, so it is made by people you know | grants: savings | identity | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: give the right to your own band's toolmakers, so it is made by hands you know | magic: sell it to the small workshop in the ward, so it is made by people you know | chance: 80
- W>R | license it for five years only, so the idea is free again after | v: self-direction, security | tribal: let the far trader make it for five summers only, and then it is free again | magic: grant the merchant house five years only, so the design is free again after | chance: 70
- U>G | license it cheaply to small makers everywhere, so it spreads on its own | aims: celebrated inventor | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: show any band's toolmaker how to make it for a small gift, so it spreads on its own | magic: grant it cheaply to small workshops everywhere, so it spreads on its own | chance: 75
- B>W | play the two firms against each other, and put the winnings in trust for the team | grants: savings | self_control: + | mark: made an enemy | v: power, benevolence | tribal: let the far trader and the near one bid against each other, and share the winnings with all who helped | magic: play the two houses against each other, and set the winnings in trust for the workshop | chance: 65
- R>U | tell them straight it is not finished, and walk away to make it better | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: tell the trader plainly the tool is not finished, and go back to the fire to make it better | magic: tell the steward plainly it is not finished, and go back to the bench to make it better | chance: 85
- G>B | wait, let the big firm come back twice, and let the price grow | self_control: + | v: power, security | tribal: wait, let the trader come back at the next two gatherings, and let the offer grow | magic: wait, let the steward call twice more, and let the price grow | chance: 60
works: The matter is settled the way {N} wanted it, and the money, the rights and the friendship are where {N} meant them to be.
works: Whatever {N} chose, a year later the invention is out in the world in some form, and {N} is working on the next one.
fails: The deal goes sour over the terms, and {N} spends the year arguing instead of making.
fails: The offer is withdrawn while {N} is still thinking, and the buyer brings out something very like it of their own.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. the prototype catches fire (calls WBR)
# A test run goes wrong in the workshop: smoke, a small fire put out at once, a burnt hand under the cold tap, nothing
# worse. [prototyping] for the rebuild and the filmed rerun, [investors who back you] for the backers asked for the next
# round, [mentor] for the old engineer down the road; aims: celebrated inventor on the cooler, simpler redesign. The
# wrong is Blue's: the fault found quietly and kept from the backers until it is fixed (hid a wrong; closed: approval;
# backfire: it fails again in front of them). Every colour's mean is 70.

== the prototype catches fire
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per inventor a year: about 1 in 4 has a test that goes wrong badly enough to stop the work for a while, most with no harm beyond a scare and a small burn (estimate); about 5 lives in 10,000 come to live from their own inventions (catalogue share)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-95
calls: WBR
holds: inventor
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: week
roles: partner, colleague, mentor
earth: a test run in the workshop goes wrong: smoke, a small fire put out at once, and a burnt hand under the cold tap
tribal: a trial of the new tool goes wrong by the fire: the pitch flares, and a burnt hand goes into the stream
magic: a trial of the new device goes wrong in the workshop: a split boiler, a small blaze, and a burnt hand in the water butt
scene: Ten minutes into the test run the prototype starts to smell, then to smoke, then a small flame runs along the casing. {N} has it out with the extinguisher in seconds, but the workshop is full of smoke and the back of {Ns} hand is sore where it touched the casing. It is nothing a cold tap and a dressing will not mend. The backers' showing is in three weeks.
scene W: There is a book of rules for the workshop, and {N} wrote half of it. Every one of them says: stop, write it down, tell people.
scene U: {N} stands in the smoke already thinking: the casing, the wiring, the heat at the joint. A failure is the most a test can ever tell anyone.
scene B: Three weeks to the showing, and the backers' money runs out in four. {N} thinks about what the backers need to hear, and what they do not.
scene R: {Ns} heart is still going hard and {N} is half laughing. It nearly worked; for nine minutes it was the best thing {N} has ever made.
scene G: The workshop is quiet again, the smoke drifting out through the open door into the garden. {N} sits on the step with a wet cloth round the hand and lets the shaking stop.
scene tribal: The new tool was to set the fire-pitch on the arrows faster, and at the trial the pitch flared up all at once along the shaft. {N} beat it out in the sand and plunged the burnt hand into the stream. The traders who backed the making with good flint come at the next moon.
scene magic: The new device was to turn the mill's stones by steam, and at the trial its little boiler hissed, split a seam and set the shavings on the floor alight. {N} smothered it with a wet sack, and stands with a burnt hand in the water butt. The merchant house that backs the work comes to see it in three weeks.
- W | stop every test, write up exactly what happened, and send it to the backers | mark: owned up | v: conformity, security | tribal: stop every trial, and tell the traders exactly what happened when they come | magic: stop every trial, write it up exactly, and send it to the merchant house | chance: 70
- U | find the fault quietly, and keep it from the backers until it is fixed | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a fault kept from the backers; backfire: it fails again, in front of them, at the showing | v: achievement, security | tribal: find the fault alone, and say nothing to the traders until it is right | magic: find the fault quietly, and keep it from the merchant house until it is mended | chance: 65
- B | show the backers how close it came, and ask for the next round now | grants: investors who back you | mark: took a wild risk | v: power, achievement | tribal: show the traders how near the tool came, and ask for more flint now | magic: show the merchant house how near it came, and ask for the next purse now | chance: 55
- R | air the workshop, rebuild it overnight, and run it again at dawn | grants: prototyping | body: light | self_control: + | v: stimulation | tribal: make the shaft again by the fire tonight, and try it again at first light | magic: air the workshop, rebuild it tonight, and fire it again at dawn | chance: 65
- G | let the workshop and the hand rest a week, and come back to it calm | self_control: + | v: security, universalism | tribal: let the hand heal and the work rest a moon, and come back to it calm | chance: 80
- W>R | fit a proper fire screen and a test bench, so it can be pushed hard safely | v: security, stimulation | tribal: clear a ring of sand to test in, so the next trial can be pushed hard safely | magic: build a stone test bench with a water tank, so it can be pushed hard safely | chance: 70
