== the prototype works
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-90
holds: software developer | civil engineer | machinist | electrician | founder of a firm | apprentice
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .03 .04 .03 .01
drivers: era+.3 fortune+.2
times: per software developer, civil engineer, machinist, electrician or apprentice of two years, or founder of a firm of one, with the knack for making things, a year: about 1 in 30 has a working model of an invention of their own; most never make a living from one: about 5 lives in 10,000 come to live from their own inventions for years, and about 2 in 1,000 hold a patent (catalogue shares; patent studies find about 1.6 in 1,000 people hold one by their thirties)
likelier: years in a trade, a workshop or a bench of one's own, good years for new machines and new ways of working, a partner who backs it, a lucky first buyer
rarer: no time or money for it, a firm that owns whatever its staff make, a lean year, a family that needs every evening
gap: 3-8
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, making, money
horizon: months
roles: partner, boss, friend, colleague, mentor
earth: a working model of an invention of one's own, on the bench at last, and the question of what to do with it
tribal: a new tool of one's own that works at last, and the question of whether to make it the work of a life
magic: a working model of a device of one's own on the bench at last, and the question of whether to live as an artificer
scene: At a quarter to midnight on a Tuesday the thing on the bench does what it was built to do, and then does it again, a hundred times, without a fault. {N} rings {partner} from the shed, unable to say anything sensible. It has taken three years of evenings. Now there is a working model, a notebook of drawings, a job to go to in the morning, and a choice.
scene W: {N} knows how this should be done: a patent filed first, every test written up, the safety marks earned before a single one is sold.
scene U: It works, and {N} can already see four ways it could work better. The model on the bench is the ninth version; the twelfth would be something else again.
scene B: For a year {N} has kept a list of everyone who might buy it, back it or copy it. The order in which they are rung matters a great deal.
scene R: {N} wants to hand in the notice tomorrow, rent a unit, and make a living from this or go broke trying.
scene G: {Ns} neighbours have watched the light in the shed for three years. The first ones, {N} thinks, should go to the people up and down the street who put up with the noise.
scene tribal: By the stream the new tool does what {N} meant it to: the hook holds every fish it takes, and the scraper bites without slipping. The others crowd round to try it. {N} could go back to the hunt as before, or become the one the bands come to for tools.
scene magic: On the bench in the back room the little device turns, and turns, and does not stop: the model {N} has worked at for three years. The master has seen it now. {N} could go back to the master's orders, or ask the guild for an artificer's mark.
