# ================================================================ fall and wrong

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. the safety test you could skip (triads D1b)
# The launch is next week and the last safety test takes a month. The wrong sits on the triad UBR (Blue's trust in its
# own numbers, Black's deadline and the orders, Red's impatience): skip it and launch on the promised day. It grants
# [savings] when it works; its closed: law line carries the backfire (it fails in use, a customer is hurt, plainly and
# offstage, and the recall takes the name), and a failed try takes [an invention in everyday use] and [name in the field]
# where they are held. The other ways: the launch delayed and the test run to the letter, a faster test that finds the
# same faults, a test house paid to run it round the clock, the thing used hard by its maker first (a plain risk, no
# harm told), the first buyers told the truth, a small signed trial, the team in shifts, a staged launch, a cheap test
# in the yard. Chances differ by act, set so every colour's mean is 70. drivers are this file's reading (stress and
# short money make the shortcut likelier, as the art pack's forgery).

== the safety test you could skip
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-95
holds: inventor | civil engineer | celebrated inventor | founder of a firm
per_year: 0 0 .01 .015 .01 .005
drivers: stress+.2 money-.2
times: per inventor, civil engineer or founder of a firm a year: about 1 in 80 faces a launch or a handover with the last safety test still to run and a reason to skip it (estimate); product recalls are common enough that most makers can name one; the four titles are about 3 lives in 100 (catalogue shares)
likelier: a date already sold to the buyers, money running short, a test that takes longer than the launch can wait, a design that has never failed so far
rarer: a firm with a safety officer who can say no, buyers who check, a maker who has lived through a recall
gap: 4-10
domain: career.5
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, partner, friend
earth: the launch is next week, and the last safety test takes a month
tribal: the bands gather in seven days to take the new tool, and its last trial takes a moon
magic: the great fair opens in a week, and the guild's last trial of the device takes a month
scene: The launch is booked for next Thursday: the first orders signed, the boxes printed, a room hired for the trade press. Then {colleague} comes in with the test plan and points at the last line. The long-run safety test, a month of use without a break, has not been done; everything else has passed. The buyers, the bank and {boss} would all rather not hear about it, and nobody would know.
scene W: {N} signed the test plan six months ago, and it says plainly what has to pass before anything is sold. A signature is a promise.
scene U: {N} looks at the results so far and runs the numbers again. The design is sound, almost certainly. Almost.
scene B: A month's delay costs the orders, the room, the bank's patience and maybe the firm. {N} weighs what it would cost to wait against what it would cost to be wrong.
scene R: {N} is sick of waiting. Every part has passed every test, the thing works, and next Thursday it is meant to go out into the world.
scene G: The first buyers are people {N} knows: a farm, a builder's yard, the hardware shop in town. {N} pictures the thing in their hands a year from now, still working.
scene tribal: In seven days the bands gather, and each has asked for the new tool. The last trial, a whole moon of hard use on the hunt, is not done, though every other trial went well. The elders want the gift ready, and nobody would know.
scene magic: The great fair opens in a week, the merchant houses have ordered, and the guild's last trial, a month of turning without a rest, has not been run. Every other trial has passed. The guild's inspector is busy, and nobody would know.
