earth: something built in the garage for the fun of it, and strangers start to ask where to get one
tribal: a new thing made by the fire for the fun of it, and people from other bands start to ask for one
magic: a small device built in a back room for the fun of it, and strangers at the market start to ask for one
scene: It began as a way to stop the garage door sticking, and now it opens jars, lifts bikes onto the wall and waters the tomatoes on a timer. {friend} filmed it for a laugh, and strangers have started asking where to buy one. {N} has a workbench, a soldering iron, a few evenings a week, and a feeling that this could be something, or nothing at all.
scene W: Nobody becomes an inventor by accident, {N} thinks. There are competitions, forms, safety marks and a proper way in, and {N} would rather take it.
scene U: {N} can see exactly what is wrong with it: the motor is too big and the timer drifts. A year of evenings would make it something else.
scene B: Strangers asking where to buy one means a market. {N} wonders who will be at the trade show this spring, and what a backer would want in return.
scene R: {N} built it laughing, and the best things are the ones built laughing. Why not just put it out there and see what happens?
scene G: The first person who asked was the old neighbour two doors down, who cannot reach the high shelves any more. {N} thinks about the street before the world.
scene tribal: It began as a way to keep the fire-sticks dry, and now the little thing of bark and sinew keeps an ember alive through a whole day's walk. The band's young ones carry it about at the camp, and people from the next band asked for one at the last meeting. {N} wonders whether it is a toy or the start of something.
scene magic: It began as a toy to amuse the household: a little brass bird that sings at sunrise. Now it opens the shutters, rings for the baker and wakes the whole lane. Strangers at the market have asked where to buy one, and a merchant's clerk has left a card.
